# Rails runtime and keys -- Rails' own rules, shipped by this layer.
#
# `rails new --skip .` writes NO .gitignore when one already exists, and in
# a clone of this template one always does: it prints `skip .gitignore` and
# every rule below is silently missing. The boilerplate then ends on
# `git add .` and `git commit`, so the project's first commit carries every
# tmp/, log/ and storage/ file the generator just produced -- measured once
# at 1779. Hence: here, BEFORE `rails new` runs, never after.
#
# ASCII ONLY, in this whole file. Rails' master-key generator rewrites
# .gitignore with `gsub!`, under the shell's encoding: a single em dash in
# here aborts `rails new` with "invalid byte sequence in US-ASCII" when the
# locale is not UTF-8. Verified both ways on Rails 8.1.3.
#
# `scripts/after_rails_new.py` checks these rules are in force and stops if
# they are not. Keep them in step with Rails' own template on an upgrade.
/.bundle
/log/*
!/log/.keep
/tmp/*
!/tmp/.keep
/tmp/pids/*
!/tmp/pids/
!/tmp/pids/.keep
/tmp/storage/*
!/tmp/storage/
!/tmp/storage/.keep
/storage/*
!/storage/.keep
/public/assets
/app/assets/builds/*
!/app/assets/builds/.keep
/vendor/bundle

# The key that decrypts config/credentials.yml.enc, and every per-
# environment key beside it. `rails new` appends this same rule itself, and
# skips it when the pattern is already there -- it is written here so that
# the key is covered even if that behaviour changes. A committed key is
# leaked once and for all: it has to be rotated at the source, not deleted.
/config/*.key

# `!.env.example` stays where the core .gitignore put it, and must remain
# the LAST .env rule of the file -- git keeps the last matching rule. The
# boilerplate appends its own `.env*` after this block, with the negation
# behind it; after_rails_new.py puts it back last if anything moves it.
